WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.560
In this video, you protect virtual machines with harvey, a command-line disaster recovery tool, and fail them over to a second cluster: first on Longhorn, then on NetApp storage.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:17.860
On the retail-store cluster, the virtual machine demo-harvey is running. Its disk is a Longhorn volume.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:29.240
harvey is a community tool, not a SUSE-supported CLI. It runs on your workstation and talks to both clusters. Run harvey protect for demo-harvey.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:35.230
harvey takes a first backup to a shared S3 target and waits until the disaster-recovery cluster restores it.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:41.860
The source volume now backs up every five minutes. On the other cluster, a standby DR volume keeps restoring each backup.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:48.480
harvey also copies the virtual machine and its cloud-init secret. The replica is created stopped.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.680
On the disaster-recovery cluster, filter the virtual machine list for demo-harvey.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.020
The replica is there, and Off. It uses no compute until you fail over.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:06.510
harvey replication status shows the DR volume in standby and up to date. Lag is the data you would lose right now.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:12.180
Now run a planned failover of demo-harvey to the disaster-recovery cluster.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:18.700
harvey stops the VM, takes a final backup, waits for its restore, then promotes the DR volume and starts the VM.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:25.830
The summary shows the total recovery time, split by phase, and a recovery point of zero seconds: no data lost.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:30.230
Back on the disaster-recovery cluster, filter for demo-harvey again.

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:35.160
The virtual machine is Running here now, on the restored Longhorn volume.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:42.460
On retail-store, the original is Off, so only one copy runs. harvey reprotect later replicates back the other way.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:53.400
harvey is not tied to Longhorn. Its project page lists three backends: NetApp SnapMirror, Portworx async DR, and Longhorn, with V2 still alpha.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:02:02.140
demo-harvey-na keeps its disk on NetApp storage. The same protect command picks the NetApp backend and mirrors the disk with SnapMirror, through Trident.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:13.410
Replication status now lists both virtual machines, each with its own backend: Longhorn for demo-harvey and NetApp for demo-harvey-na.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:21.210
The planned failover runs the same phases: stop, a final SnapMirror update, promote the replica volume, and start the VM.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:26.820
The summary again shows the recovery time by phase and a recovery point of zero seconds.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:31.540
On the disaster-recovery cluster, filter the list for demo-harvey once more.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:38.140
Both virtual machines run here now, one on a Longhorn volume and one on a NetApp volume.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:47.979
Both virtual machines now run on the disaster-recovery cluster, with no data lost. Next, you fail over a VM with NetApp Trident Protect.
